<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What causes a bump inside the mouth on the gum?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">A bump on the gum near a tooth is most commonly a gumboil (dental abscess) — a pus-filled swelling caused by infection at the tooth root tip. Other possibilities include a periodontal abscess from gum disease, a benign fibrous epulis, or a mucocele if on the inner lip. An X-ray at CARE Hospitals determines whether the cause is dental or periodontal.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Should I be concerned about an intraoral swelling that doesn't hurt?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Yes — a painless intraoral swelling that persists for more than 2–3 weeks requires evaluation. While many intraoral swellings are infections (which eventually become painful), some are cystic or neoplastic lesions that may not cause pain until late stages. Our surgeons perform biopsy on persistent painless intraoral swellings to rule out serious pathology.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can a swelling inside the mouth be cancer?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">While many intraoral swellings are benign (mucoceles, fibromas, torus), any persistent swelling, ulcer, or mass in the mouth that lasts more than 3 weeks should be evaluated with a biopsy. Oral squamous cell carcinoma can present as a painless swelling or ulcer on the tongue, floor of mouth, or gums. Early detection dramatically improves outcomes.</div>
</details>
